  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The town of La Londe-les-Maures has set up a small water-entry area for dogs, in front of the former Bormettes factory, a converted industrial site that gives the place its name. Two other beaches in the town — Pellegrin and Argentière — also tolerate dogs on leash, outside the supervised swimming areas.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>A point of ambiguity worth flagging: several independent sources actually place La Londe's "dog beach" instead between Tamaris beach and Argentière beach (facing a disused military building), with coordinates noticeably different from the ones used here — it could be the same site under a different local name, or two separate areas. In summer (15 June-15 September), one source states that dogs' swimming there would be limited to 6am-9am and 7pm-10pm, with no such restriction the rest of the year — information not confirmed by a second source, worth checking with the town hall. One unconfirmed signal, also not cross-checked, suggests that from June 2026 dogs may no longer be accepted on Argentière beach: as a precaution, check on-site before going there specifically for that. As on most beaches in this area, the leash and respect for supervised swimming areas remain the rule.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: if Bormettes is too crowded, Argentière beach a little further east is a good alternative.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Cani Plouf in Le Pradet is about a twenty-minute drive away, for a change of scenery.</p>
